  "answer_text": "The iconic scene of Del Boy falling through the bar is from the episode **\"Yuppy Love\"**! Here are the key details:\n\n### \ud83d\udcfa The Episode\n**\"Yuppy Love\"** was the first episode of Series 6 of *Only Fools and Horses*, airing on **8th January 1989** on BBC1, attracting a viewing figure of **13.9 million**.\n\n### \ud83c\udf78 The Scene\nBack at the wine bar, Del and Trigger's attention is caught by two women who smile at them. The barman lifts up the bar flap, but Del is unaware of this. He tells Trig they may be in luck and to play it nice and cool. As he goes to lean on the bar, he falls right over. Trigger looks around wondering where Del has gone, and Del gets up and says they are leaving.\n\n### \ud83c\udfad Behind the Scenes\n- The scene was inspired by writer John Sullivan watching a real man do the exact same thing in a wine bar \u2014 except the man grabbed onto the fixed part of the bar so he didn't fall all the way over. Sullivan thought it was funny for the man's body language as he tried to recover his cool.\n\n- Sullivan originally wanted a slip, stumble, and a tree-like fall, but David Jason thought Del should go all the way over \u2014 starting to go sideways and then falling without looking in the direction of the fall, which Jason believed was the key to the scene. There was a hidden crash mat, but it was a difficult shot because it was hard not to look where he was falling.\n\n- A rehearsal script that went up for auction revealed that **Trigger wasn't supposed to be in the scene originally**. However, Roger Lloyd Pack happened to be there on the day, and John Sullivan decided he should be included after all.\n\n- Roger Lloyd Pack (Trigger) later revealed that David Jason never did the fall in rehearsal, so his shocked reaction was genuinely a surprise \u2014 he had to keep a straight face under enormous pressure in the studio.\n\n### \ud83c\udfc6 Legacy\nDel Boy's fall through the bar has become one of the most celebrated sitcom scenes ever. David Jason says people ask him about that fall all the time, and some never like to talk about anything else \u2014 but he's happy to be remembered for something so iconic.",
